-- title:
--   Intermediate subfields of a one-variable function field are curves
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $\Omega$ be fields (both in `Type`), with $\Omega$ a $K$-algebra and $K$ algebraically closed, and let $E \le F$ be intermediate fields of $\Omega/K$. Assume given an element $t \in \Omega$ lying in $E$ and transcendental over $K$, and assume that $F$ is a one-variable function field in the sense that there exists $x \in F$ transcendental over $K$ such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. The conclusion is threefold. First, [`AlgebraicCurve.IsCurveOver K E`](def/AlgebraicCurve_IsCurveOver.html#L15) holds, i.e. (i) for every nonzero $f \in E$ there is a divisor $D$ on $E/K$ with $D(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v(f)$ at every place $v$ and $\deg D = 0$, where a place of $E/K$ is a valuation subring of $E$ containing the image of $K$, different from $E$ itself, and a principal ideal ring; (ii) the residue field of every such place is a finite $K$-module; and (iii) the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega[E/K]$ is free of rank one over $E$. Second, $E$ is essentially of finite type over $K$. Third, there exists $y \in E$ transcendental over $K$ with $E$ finite-dimensional over $K(y)$.
--
--   This is the statement that a subfield $E$ of a one-variable function field $F/K$ ($K$ algebraically closed) which contains a transcendental element is itself a one-variable function field, hence a curve over $K$ in the divisor-theoretic sense used here; it is packaged for the case where $E$ and $F$ sit inside a common ambient field $\Omega$. It feeds the integrality statements for adjoined residues of $j$ and $q$ on modular curves of full level, where the component fields occur as intermediate fields of a field of Laurent series.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_isCurveOver_and_essFiniteType_of_le_of_transcendental_mem.lean

